course: dictionary
lesson: 14446
title: Panicked: when fear takes over
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Panicked means you suddenly felt such strong fear or worry that you could not think clearly. Look at the words around it to see what happened.
explain: «When the lights went out, Sam panicked and could not find the door.» The lights going out was a sudden shock, and not finding the door shows that Sam could not think clearly. So panicked means Sam lost his calm.
explain: Panicked can also describe a voice, a look or a crowd: «She had a panicked look on her face.» Here panicked is an adjective, so it describes a noun. Check by swapping in scared: if it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: Panic is the root word; panicked is its past tense (panic, panics, panicked, panicking).
fact: «Panicked» means suddenly felt such strong fear or worry that you could not think clearly.
fact: As an adjective, «panicked» describes something that shows panic, like «a panicked voice».
fact: Safety rule: panicking makes things worse, so stay calm, follow the plan and find an adult.
fact: In everyday talk, people say they panicked about a test or a lost key.
exercise: «When the fire alarm rang, the children panicked and ran in all directions.» What does panicked mean? | *Felt strong fear so they could not think clearly | Sang a happy song together | Felt very sleepy and wanted a nap
exercise: «Stay calm, don't panic, and find an adult you trust.» What is the safest thing to do? | *Stay calm and find an adult you trust | Hide under the desk and wait for hours | Shout and run around in circles
exercise: Which word in «The panicked crowd pushed towards the door» tells you how the crowd felt? | door | towards | pushed | *panicked
test: «Mia panicked when she lost her house key.» What does panicked mean here? | *Suddenly felt strong fear and could not think clearly | Felt calm and happy | Fell asleep on the step | Calmly looked for a pen
test: Which is the best advice when you feel panicked? | Shout and run around | Ignore the problem and go to sleep | *Take a breath, stay calm and find an adult | Hide your phone and say nothing
exam: Which sentence uses panicked as an adjective, describing a noun? | They panicked about the school trip | The horse was calm and quiet | *The panicked horse kicked the gate | He panicked when the bell rang
